DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  nnll__llaannggiinnffoo()  and nnll__llaannggiinnffoo__ll() functions provide access to lo‐
       cale information in a more flexible way than  llooccaalleeccoonnvv(3).   nnll__llaanngg‐‐
       iinnffoo() returns a string which is the value corresponding to _i_t_e_m in the
       program’s  current  global  locale.   nnll__llaannggiinnffoo__ll()  returns a string
       which is the value corresponding to _i_t_e_m for the locale  identified  by
       the locale object _l_o_c_a_l_e, which was previously created by nneewwllooccaallee(3).
       Individual  and  additional  elements  of  the locale categories can be
       queried.

       Examples for the locale elements that can be specified  in  _i_t_e_m  using
       the constants defined in _<_l_a_n_g_i_n_f_o_._h_> are:

       CCOODDEESSEETT (LC_CTYPE)
              Return  a string with the name of the character encoding used in
              the  selected  locale,  such  as   "UTF-8",   "ISO-8859-1",   or
              "ANSI_X3.4-1968"  (better  known as US‐ASCII).  This is the same
              string that you get with "locale charmap".  For a list of  char‐
              acter encoding names, try "locale -m" (see llooccaallee(1)).

       DD__TT__FFMMTT (LC_TIME)
              Return  a  string  that can be used as a format string for ssttrrff‐‐
              ttiimmee(3) to represent time and date in a locale‐specific way  (%%cc
              conversion specification).

       DD__FFMMTT (LC_TIME)
              Return  a  string  that can be used as a format string for ssttrrff‐‐
              ttiimmee(3) to represent a date in a locale‐specific way (%%xx conver‐
              sion specification).

       TT__FFMMTT (LC_TIME)
              Return a string that can be used as a format  string  for  ssttrrff‐‐
              ttiimmee(3) to represent a time in a locale‐specific way (%%XX conver‐
              sion specification).

       AAMM__SSTTRR (LC_TIME)
              Return  a string that represents affix for ante meridiem (before
              noon, "AM") time.  (Used in %%pp ssttrrffttiimmee(3) conversion specifica‐
              tion.)

       PPMM__SSTTRR (LC_TIME)
              Return a string that represents affix for post meridiem  (before
              midnight, "PM") time.  (Used in %%pp ssttrrffttiimmee(3) conversion speci‐
              fication.)

       TT__FFMMTT__AAMMPPMM (LC_TIME)
              Return  a  string  that can be used as a format string for ssttrrff‐‐
              ttiimmee(3) to represent a time in a.m. or p.m. notation  in  a  lo‐
              cale‐specific way (%%rr conversion specification).

       EERRAA (LC_TIME)
              Return  era  description,  which  contains information about how
              years are counted and displayed for each era in a locale.   Each
              era description segment shall have the format:

                     _d_i_r_e_c_t_i_o_n:_o_f_f_s_e_t:_s_t_a_r_t___d_a_t_e:_e_n_d___d_a_t_e:_e_r_a___n_a_m_e:_e_r_a___f_o_r_m_a_t

              according to the definitions below:

              _d_i_r_e_c_t_i_o_n   Either a "++" or a "‐‐" character.  The "++" means that
                          years  increase  from  the  _s_t_a_r_t___d_a_t_e  towards  the
                          _e_n_d___d_a_t_e, "‐‐" means the opposite.

              _o_f_f_s_e_t      The epoch year of the _s_t_a_r_t___d_a_t_e.

              _s_t_a_r_t___d_a_t_e  A date in the form _y_y_y_y/_m_m/_d_d, where _y_y_y_y,  _m_m,  and
                          _d_d are the year, month, and day numbers respectively
                          of the start of the era.

              _e_n_d___d_a_t_e    The  ending  date  of the era, in the same format as
                          the _s_t_a_r_t___d_a_t_e, or one of  the  two  special  values
                          "‐‐**" (minus infinity) or "++**" (plus infinity).

              _e_r_a___n_a_m_e    The  name of the era, corresponding to the %%EECC ssttrrff‐‐
                          ttiimmee(3) conversion specification.

              _e_r_a___f_o_r_m_a_t  The format of the year in the era, corresponding  to
                          the %%EEYY ssttrrffttiimmee(3) conversion specification.

              Era  description segments are separated by semicolons.  Most lo‐
              cales do not define this value.  Examples of locales that do de‐
              fine this value are the Japanese and Thai locales.

       EERRAA__DD__TT__FFMMTT (LC_TIME)
